Oh, boy!  David has summarized our discussion for us.  Since David
knows everything, this summary must be *very* informative.  :-)

David Hairston writes:

>fact: pools _are_ hazardous.
>question: is it playable?

You have missed the main point of the discussion, Dave.  Many people
discussed whether nethack should be a game of strategy vs. a game of
hand-eye coordination (you may have called this "alertness").

>personally, i don't think the game should pander to unresponsive
>interfaces.

The user interface is part of nethack.  The complaint is about
nethack's user interface.  Why don't you understand?

> i like the
>deadly threat of pools.  for those that have won the game it is a feature
>that commands respect.

How can you claim to know what all winners think?  You don't seem to
have even followed the discussion.  Winners may not go wading, but
this winner does not respect pools.  "Resent" is more like it.

>if confirmation is used consider
>the fact that currently there is no motivation to enter water

This issue was never discussed, though a knowledgeable guy like you
should be aware of at least three reasons for intentionally entering a
square with water.

(To avoid spoilers, close your eyes, hit space, wait for rn to flush
typeahead, and hit space again.)

(1) The first time, a player won't know that it is deadly and might
sincerely be trying to cross.  Why would it look any worse than
attacking a peaceful monster?

(2) A player who can teleport might just want to see what's on the
other side (heh, heh).

(3) A player who is levitating or flying would certainly want to
cross.

> and so
>the only response will be "no" and the method's utility will only be
>to prevent deaths by drowning.

Suppose that there were a 1 in 10 chance of finding something useful
in the water and not dying.  Are you saying that confirmation would be
OK in this case?  But you say confirmation is not OK if water is
always deadly?  I guess there is some logic behind that, but it is
beyond the capabilities of my little human brain.  Again, I think that
you are ignoring the main point of discussion: do we want nethack to
be more a strategy game or more a hand-eye coordination game?

> that doesn't seem to be in the spirit
>of the game.

I think you mean to say that you like the hand-eye coordination aspect
of the game.  Why don't you speak for yourself instead of claiming to
know the true "spirit" of nethack?

> i would sincerely hope
>this feature is not eliminated merely because it extremely hazardous.

Has anyone seriously proposed eliminating pools?  I don't think so.
The most serious proposals would just make it harder to accidentally
step into one.

I would personally be happy if water-filled areas became more dangerous.
(spoilers.  two spaces again)

In hack I recall being pulled into the water by a giant eel.

I don't
know if nethack has this feature.  This could certainly be beefed up.
Squares near water could be made slippery (possibly in the form of
a trap).  Stepping on a slip trap could slide you into the moat.  Fighting
on a slippery square could entail a chance of falling in (or being pushed
in by a large enough monster).  The eel might be replaced by sea monsters
that have a high chance of pulling you in.  Then I would respect moats.  Right
now I just resent them.

The preceding remarks do not apply to pools formed by fountains.
Unless the fountain overflows into a pit, it is hard to imagine the
pool being very deep.

I can't estimate how hard this would be to code up.  I also thing that
it is orthogonal to the discussion of whether to request confirmation,
which I favor for water-filled areas user-specified monsters, among other
things.

On a real tangent, how about making food really dangerous?

The Bubbie-Troll hits.--More--
She force-feeds you a blue jelly donut--More--
You feel kind of gross.  Stop eating? [yn]y
You can't!--More--
